  {
    "episode_id": 49869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Brooklyn has its own brand of political tactics and strategies. Look, I grew up in the quintessential middle-class household. My parents moved to Levitown when I was born. Levitown was America's first suburb. Tiny little house, all they can afford. They did it on the GI Bill. My dad returned from the army, served in Alaska. And, you know, we grew up in a fairly comfortable community. And in the 1970s, when I was just becoming a teenager, the economy kind of turned and suddenly the things that we were used to, the vacations and things like that became tougher and tougher for my parents to afford. My mom opened up a typing service in the den. People may not remember what a typewriter is, but there used to be such a thing. And so I would watch like the Brady Bunch on television next to the clacking and clicking of my mom's typewriter. And that's where I kind of got this sense of fairness to the middle class. You know, I went to a community college because I couldn't afford. My family couldn't afford George Washington University. So I went to Nassau Community College and then that prepared me to go to George Washington University. And I did it mostly on student financial assistance or federal loans. And so everything that I did in Congress and in politics geared towards middle-class security and prosperity came out of my own DNA.",
