  {
    "episode_id": 49869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "So actually, before I went to work for former Congressman Aunger, I was a part-time get this another kind of antiquated title, computer operator for Congressman Bob Matsui from Sacramento, California. Oh, I didn't know that. Yeah, so I would go to class. I would go to GW during the day and then show up in Congressman Matsui's office in the Cannon House office building at about six and enter addresses from constituent mail for three or four hours and then leave, go have dinner and prepare for the next day. So I wasn't, you know, wasn't a legislative correspondent, but I was the person who in those days, this is in, I guess, 1982, 81 or 82, back in those days, you know, you had to tediously enter constituent addresses and code it by issue. So I started there, and then I heard about an opening for Congressman Dick Ottinger from Westchester in New York. I was a New Yorker. I always had an interest in foreign policy and national security, and I applied for a job as a legislative assistant, got that job, and stayed there for three years, and everything, everything that Dick Ottinger taught me. was responsible for my election to Congress many years later. I think it was about 17 years later. Say one other thing about this, it was really kind of a surreal moment when I was sworn in to the United States Congress, January 3, 2001, raised my right hand, took my oath to the Constitution, and the first person to shake my hand was Congressman Bob Metzoui, who was still serving. I went to work for him in his freshman year. He was still serving when I was elected. Very tragically, he died several years later. But that moment, shaking hands with my former boss, my first boss on Capitol Hill, was a moment that I will always remember.",
