    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "would have been in Congress without you. And he responded, I'm happy to take some of the credit, which is very nice of him. Dick Ottinger, he was a tough boss. He was kind of a stickler for detail. So if you wrote a dear colleague letter, And the phrasing was off or you made or you had a grammatical error or a misspelling, he would return it to you with the word sloppy, scroll to top it. And I used to fear every morning coming in and getting a submission back from him that he didn't like. On the other hand, if he liked what you did, He would write, and I could see it in front of me right now, so vividly, well done. And I collected all those well-duns. They were so meaningful to me. So what I learned from DeGottinger was detail counts, how you express yourself as an elected official is very important, and accuracy matters. You have to check the facts. You can't have a member of Congress that you're working for say something to be embarrassed later. That kind of, it was like a conventional, almost grammar school teaching gave me the discipline that I needed to make sure that later in life, as I was expressing myself or writing as a member of Congress, that I was doing it in a detailed, authentic, and accurate way.",
