  {
    "episode_id": 49869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "So Dick Ottinger taught me about the importance of public service, and particularly the influence that a member of Congress or that member of staff can have. He was a very empowering boss, and so we were required every morning as a condition of our employment to read the New York Times. And if we saw something that bothered us, something that we thought could be changed, We were just able to do it. He had to approve everything, so I remember quite vividly. Ronald Reagan decided to, this must have been in 1983, to deploy troops to El Salvador. And I was reading New York Times' story about that this might be a potential violation of the War Powers Act. uh... and i did a memo to take on your saying the time says that this may be a potential violation of the war powers act i think we ought to uh... organized so some of your colleagues uh... around legislation that would stop this uh... and his response was go for it uh... and we did it uh... and i learned from him that members of congress can change policy, and their staffs can do the same. That's when I decided that I wanted to be a member of Congress myself. So I moved back to New York to run. I didn't think it would take me 17 years to actually get elected to Congress. And I didn't realize the importance of just damn good luck in setting up the circumstances where you can run and win. But I went home. I decided I needed to learn what it's like to be a candidate. I ran in a Democrat. I ran in a brutally, brutally Republican district. It was a Tea Party district like 27 years before, 20 years before the Tea Party. And I put myself through it. I was running against an incumbent Suffolk County legislature who, at a debate, informed me as we shook hands that she was carrying a gun. It was a rough debate. And I",
