    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "So I look, I don't know if I had a leg up on my colleagues, but my approach to legislation was forged and fashioned as a staffer on Capitol Hill. I had a sense of of the process, first of all, you know, I knew how you do a dear colleague. I knew how you have to reach across the aisle and find a Republican who will work with you on an issue. So I had a sense of a practical sense of the intricacies of the legislative process on Capitol Hill, and that was clearly an advantage. I didn't have to learn it at freshman orientation. I did it for several years. That was the first. The second thing was, I learned the practical skill of relating legislation to constituent needs. And so, Congressman Ottinger was very, very, you know, everything we did had to reflect his values, but also had to have resonance with his constituents. And so being a staffer taught me how to relate the two, how to blend the two, good policy, but also something that constituents care about.",
