  {
    "episode_id": 49869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, like I made a ton of them. I think in the case of working for Dick Gottinger, There was nothing, you know, cosmic, no universal screw up. It was silly mistakes. So I remember I wrote a speech for him where I somehow confused Bolivia and Brazil. Now, it was caught by the Chief of Staff before it went to Congressman Atenger. But the fact that I can still remember this mistake, which happened in 1981 in 2020, should give you a sense of how it affected me. It was just a dumb mistake. I wasn't thinking and not catastrophic, but I learned from that that you just got to double check everything. Can you imagine if this congressman had read this speech and talked about Brazil instead of Bolivia. It just would have been horrible, horrible. And so I realized the role of a staffer is so vitally important. You put words in the principal's mouth, and if those words are wrong, that's a problem.",
