  {
    "episode_id": 49869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 71,
    "turn_text": "Sure, I think first and foremost, just how crushing the job can be. So in many congressional offices, it's fairly stovepipe. You've got the press, the comms director, you've got the chief of staff, you've got the senior LA, the LA's who are working on specific portfolio of issues, the scheduler. And in many offices, they don't have a sense of how it all bears down on the member. in terms of time and demands. They may not be aware of the members' family commitments. They may not be aware of the members, you know, just a personal interest that a member may have in focusing on a specific issue. Sometimes they may not be aware of the fact that the member has to eat. I remember calling I scheduled at once and, you know, I was a little bit annoying. I called her and I said, yeah, I'm just curious, do you? Do you want lunch? And of course she assumed that I was inviting her to lunch. And she said, oh, Congressman, that's so nice. I said, so you want lunch? And she said, yes, I do. And I said, well, so would I, every once in a while. Could you please put on a schedule?",
