  {
    "episode_id": 49869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "Well, you must be a glutton for punishment because in addition to being an elected official, a member of the House from a competitive district, you also served as DCCC chairman for two terms. And I think as anyone knows, who's served on Capitol Hill or Ben on Capitol Hill, You know, your political acumen is well known. And I think anyone who runs the D-Trip or the DS or the Republican counterparts, it's very important that their colleagues have trust in their political skill because they're not, you know, they're working for everybody. In 2012, you picked up eight seats in a very difficult redistricted environment. 2014 was a difficult year, a midterm election for a second term president and the tide swung back and Republicans ended up gaining a very large majority. Tell me about what do you say to the staff after a defeat like that that is so environmental, number one. So many things are outside of your control. And yet, people are working nonstop for years as if their actions that day will make things incrementally better. So it's such a contrast with intensity of effort, which does have an impact, and yet there's still a large portion of elections that are outside of a campaign's control. Tell me how you dealt with that in that moment.",
