  {
    "episode_id": 49869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 79,
    "turn_text": "Well, first of all, I think you're absolutely right that you're chairing one of the political committees is basically you are trying to figure out how to exploit a weather forecast or defend against a weather forecast. It is entirely environmental. And so when we picked up those eight seats, I got much more credit than I deserved. And when we lost seats, I probably got more criticism than I deserved. When Rahm Emanuel won the house. He did an exceptional job. He figured out how to exploit the environment. Chris Van Hollen, we had that horrible 2010. There's nothing that he could have done that would have changed that environment. Nothing. But what you're doing is you're managing weather forecasts. More to your question, you know, it's a, you know, look, you're going to political combat and you develop the, the camaraderie that anybody who's been in that kind of combat develops. And you've got to understand that you're not going to win every battle. You, as long as you have, and I've had this conversation with D-Trip staff and others, if you did everything that you were supposed to do and nothing that you weren't supposed to do, you did as well as you could. In other words, don't bemoan a loss where you executed perfectly. If you lost because you made a mistake, that's a problem. I've never been involved in a race either at D-Trip or anywhere else where the loss was a result of an error. The loss was a result of running against a superior candidate being outspent those external variables. And I think that's an important lesson for political staffers. Put a campaign together, develop your strategy, execute, be tactical and strategic. If at the end of the day you still lost as long as it wasn't something that you contributed to, then you did well.",
