    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 98,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, that was a really, really important question. So when I was in Congress, I valued what I called utility players. This goes back to my love of the Mets. I loved somebody who was a legislative assistant but also understood what skills were necessary in order to pursue a press angle. Or having a comms director who was utility player and could understand if she saw, for example, a constituent services when back home getting a veteran retroactive payment how to turn that into. a press conference or legislation. So having that Swiss Army knife ability, being able to pull out whatever tool you need to address a problem or to advance an issue is indispensable. And we try to train our staff to have those skills. And in fact, we cross-trained quite a few. We would routinely bring DC staffers to the district office. I think a lot of members of Congress do this to shadow a constituent services representative or the outreach director. And we bring our district staff to Washington to shadow the LAs so that they could kind of sharpen those utility tools.",
