    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 108,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, I grew up in that house in Levittown with three dreams. One was one day being elected to Congress. The other was being a novelist. And the third was playing left field for the New York Mets. And since I sucked at Little League, I had to disqualify my baseball career. And I'm really lucky, you know, two of those dreams came true. Writing has always been my therapy. You know, everybody needs kind of a release. Congressional staffers need a release. Members of Congress need a release. My release was writing. And I would notice something on the floor of the house or in the Democratic caucus. And it just began to reflect on it and weave it into a storyline. So I did the Global War on Morris, which is about actually Dick Cheney and the Global War on Terror, based on conversations I had with Cheney and President Bush and the leadership of the House and my own constituents. and it did very well and as you said was option to rob riner by the way when you option a book to hollywood it's the equipment functional quivalent of the bill being referred to committee it means next",
